-- What the STM32H753 runs from its reset vector, before compiler-owned
-- reset writes any memory (D653).  Written by hand from RM0433 Rev 9.
--
-- After a power-on reset the system is in Run*, and until the supply
-- configuration is written to PWR_CR3 and PWR_CSR1's ACTVOSRDY says the
-- voltage levels are valid, write accesses to the RAMs are not permitted
-- (6.4.1, step 5a, p. 262).  Reset copies the initialized data and clears
-- the zeroed into the AXI SRAM, so the supply is configured here first: the
-- LDO, the H753's only regulator (Table 34, p. 261), LDOEN set with BYPASS
-- and SCUEN clear in PWR_CR3's write-once lower byte (6.8.4, p. 308), and
-- ACTVOSRDY, PWR_CSR1's bit 13 (6.8.2, p. 305), waited for at most a
-- million polls.  The stage uses registers alone: no stack and no RAM.  A
-- supply that never becomes valid leaves the core at supply_stalled, where
-- a debugger finds it, rather than in reset writing a RAM it may not.
link(boot_stage) extern(naked) supply: () -> none =
    assembler.block("""
        ldr r0, =0x58024800     @ PWR_BASE (stm32h753xx.h)
        ldr r1, [r0, #12]       @ PWR_CR3
        movs r2, #7             @ SCUEN, LDOEN and BYPASS
        bics r1, r2
        movs r2, #2             @ LDOEN
        orrs r1, r2
        str r1, [r0, #12]
        ldr r2, =1000000
        supply_wait:
        ldr r1, [r0, #4]        @ PWR_CSR1
        lsls r1, r1, #18        @ ACTVOSRDY, bit 13, to the sign
        bmi supply_ready
        subs r2, #1
        bne supply_wait
        supply_stalled:
        b supply_stalled
        supply_ready:
        ldr r0, =_landin_firmware_reset
        bx r0
        """)
end supply
